ar school-project style. You can copy this directly into your notebook.</p>
<hr data-start="174" data-end="177">
<h2 data-start="179" data-end="237"><strong data-start="182" data-end="237">Page 13: Case Studies – Health and Economic Poverty</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="239" data-end="259"><strong data-start="243" data-end="259">Case Study 1</strong></h3>
<p data-start="260" data-end="684">The first case study is of a middle-aged daily wage worker suffering from frequent illness and body weakness. Due to poor health, he is unable to work regularly, which directly affects his income. His low earnings prevent him from accessing proper medical treatment and nutritious food. This case clearly shows how poor health leads to poverty, and how poverty further worsens health conditions, creating a continuous cycle.</p>
<h3 data-start="686" data-end="706"><strong data-start="690" data-end="706">Case Study 2</strong></h3>
<p data-start="707" data-end="1103">The second case study involves a woman working in the unorganised sector who suffers from anaemia and chronic fatigue. Her limited income does not allow her to consult doctors regularly or purchase medicines. As a result, her health condition remains untreated. In this case, poverty is the main cause of poor health, as lack of financial resources restricts access to healthcare and proper diet.</p>
<h3 data-start="1105" data-end="1125"><strong data-start="1109" data-end="1125">Case Study 3</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1126" data-end="1530">The third case study is of an elderly person who has no stable source of income and depends on family members for support. Due to financial constraints, medical check-ups are often delayed or avoided. His worsening health further increases dependence on others, reducing his ability to contribute economically. This case highlights how poverty and poor health are closely linked and reinforce each other.</p>
<h3 data-start="1532" data-end="1559"><strong data-start="1536" data-end="1559">Overall Observation</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1560" data-end="1955">From the above case studies, it is evident that poor health and economic poverty are interrelated. In most cases, poverty leads to poor health due to inadequate nutrition, sanitation, and healthcare facilities. At the same time, poor health reduces a person’s earning capacity, pushing them further into poverty. Thus, health and poverty form a vicious cycle that needs to be addressed together.</p>
<hr data-start="1957" data-end="1960">
